WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:05.410
It’s such a beautiful day out here. The
air is alive with birdsong and the gentle

00:00:05.410 --> 00:00:12.490
bubbling of nearby brooks. There are ancient
oaks everywhere, reaching up into the sky

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:19.610
and forming a dense canopy. The occasional
shaft of light spears through amongst the

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:25.539
leaves, illuminating the lush, green moss
on the ground below, as soft and perfect underfoot

00:00:25.539 --> 00:00:30.359
as a brand new rug. This might seem like we’re
just having a pleasant jaunt through a local

00:00:30.359 --> 00:00:35.370
forest here at SCP Explained, but take a closer
look at the trees.

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:42.260
This isn’t just any forest, dear viewers.
That’s right: After years of attempted espionage

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:49.170
and infiltration, we’re here at long last:
The Wanderer’s Library - The limitless,

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:54.640
interdimensional archive of all knowledge
in the multiverse, often frequented by the

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:59.640
Serpent’s Hand via portals known as The
Ways. Who would have guessed that the secret

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:06.210
to getting inside was just asking very politely
and promising not to steal any of the books?

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:09.150
We should try just being nice more often…

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:14.400
But hey, as the dispensers of user-friendly
animated knowledge here on YouTube, we felt

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:20.090
that we ought to share this bounty of limitless
knowledge with you. The wing of the Wanderer’s

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:24.509
Library we’re seeing right now is known
as the Hidden Nest, it’s a good starting

00:01:24.509 --> 00:01:30.100
point for new scholars perusing the boundless
texts available beyond dimensions. How about

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:35.899
we begin with this collection over here: King
Shash’s Shelf. Try saying that three times

00:01:35.899 --> 00:01:39.810
fast, and then take a look at the inscription
above the case:

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:47.400
“The lord of the lazy, his kingdom is anywhere
in which sloth reigns above activity, in the

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:54.609
hearts of all those devote to lounging over
labor. He and his secret army wage a war against

00:01:54.609 --> 00:02:00.770
all do-ers, a battle for the universe that
can only end in the cessation of all motion

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:03.929
in the universe or total, unstoppable chaos.”

00:02:03.929 --> 00:02:10.140
Lord of the Lazy, hm? Sounds like me when
I clock off for the weekend. Let’s crack

00:02:10.140 --> 00:02:16.150
the spines on a few of these dusty old volumes
and take a look inside. First, we have “On

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:22.019
The Common Vampyre”, an extract from the
thought-to-be-lost Saint-Emile's Bestiary,

00:02:22.019 --> 00:02:27.620
translated into English by the famed scholar
of the anomalous known as Rounderhouse. It

00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:32.900
is an indispensable source of knowledge for
those who are aware of, and rightly fear,

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.950
the vampires that lurk in the dark all around
us.

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:42.110
It goes into great detail about the biology
of the Common Vampire - also known as Vampyrae

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:47.909
Draculae, and believed to be the progenitor
to all other vampire species - as well as

00:02:47.909 --> 00:02:53.290
their diet, behavior, and the best methods
of hunting them. The following is a brief

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:56.650
extract.
“Vampyrae draculae is largely similar in

00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:03.260
Shape and Forme to Homo sapiens sapiens - an
erect biped, with a wingspan roughly equivalent

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:09.030
to its height. The most obvious feature discerning
them from a Human is the shape of their hands

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:15.280
— the Vampyre's hands are claw-like, with
long, hard nails extending up to two centimetres,

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:19.819
often sharpened to a point. The muscles in
these hands are taut and powerful — they

00:03:19.819 --> 00:03:25.150
can be quickly contracted to exert immense
pressure on anything in the Vampyre's grasp.

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:29.640
This ability is commonly used to crush the
skulls or bones of prey.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:35.250
Naturally, the Vampyre's teeth are the second
source of notability. The Fangs of a Vampyre

00:03:35.250 --> 00:03:41.150
are stuff of legend, and it is difficult to
extricate myth from fact. Dissection of the

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:46.670
adult Specimens have noted sexual dimorphism
in the dental region — while males have

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:52.010
two-score-and-eight teeth to the females'
two-score, they are less dangerous - four

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:57.200
of the males' can be described as Fangs, both
front-facing, two on top and two on bottom,

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:03.040
each a centimetre above the gumline. Comparatively,
females have eight Fangs, four in front and

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:09.239
two on each side. Her front Fangs are up to
one and a half centimetre above the gumline.

00:04:09.239 --> 00:04:14.280
The legends have preserved one important bit
of truth regarding the Fangs; they are used

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:19.889
almost exclusively to suck Blood from the
Vampyre's prey. They bite into the flesh,

00:04:19.889 --> 00:04:25.310
most often of the neck or thigh, and the Vampyre
uses its lips to create a seal around the

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:32.090
incisions before sucking. However, study of
their feeding habits has led me to an intriguing

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:36.919
discovery: as soon as the beast separates
itself from its prey, its Saliva soothes the

00:04:36.919 --> 00:04:42.720
wound and encourages faster clotting. While
this is pure speculation, your author theorizes

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:47.810
that this is an Adaptation to preserve the
life of the prey in order to remain a consistent

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:48.810
food source.”

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:54.420
A truly fascinating piece of technical literature.
Next, of course, there is the short but haunting

00:04:54.420 --> 00:05:00.930
piece titled “Leavings Of Another World”,
written by the great thinker Jack Manganese.

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:05.000
This tome carries an important lesson to all
who wander through the hallowed halls of the

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:10.300
Wanderer’s Library about the power of words.
But, it’s not the kind of power you might

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:14.889
think. Allow this extract to expand on that.
“Words have power.

00:05:14.889 --> 00:05:21.630
Indeed, they have the power to create, but
mostly they have the power to destroy. Does

00:05:21.630 --> 00:05:27.849
that really surprise you? Let me demonstrate.
Please think of an animal, any animal. It

00:05:27.849 --> 00:05:34.300
could be anything, right? Two-legged? Four-legged?
Winged? Anything. But, now suppose I said

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:39.889
"Think of a quadruped". It can no longer be
two-legged, can it? It can no longer be a

00:05:39.889 --> 00:05:41.539
bird or a fish.

00:05:41.539 --> 00:05:49.069
And if I said, "Think of a black, domesticated
feline", your choices are narrower still.

00:05:49.069 --> 00:05:56.360
And finally, "Think of Bastet, my pet black
cat"? At that point, you have no choices at

00:05:56.360 --> 00:06:01.379
all. You may believe that each step gives
you more information, but what it really does

00:06:01.379 --> 00:06:09.080
is limit your imagination and destroy possibilities.
The power to destroy, you see, is much greater

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:15.121
than the power to create. You just didn't
notice because you, yourself, are a creature

00:06:15.121 --> 00:06:22.050
of thoughts, ideas, language, and ultimately
of words.

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:28.509
Suppose I told you there was once a world
without language, without ideas, without words.

00:06:28.509 --> 00:06:34.699
Of course "world" itself is a word, so it
wasn't really a world, but we have to call

00:06:34.699 --> 00:06:36.449
it something, now don't we?

00:06:36.449 --> 00:06:43.810
In this world there were no limitations. Everything
that could be, was. Everything that couldn't

00:06:43.810 --> 00:06:50.880
be, also was. It was a vast place of infinite
complexity, but also of infinite simplicity.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:55.250
Since everything that was or wasn't, also
was or wasn't everything else, the endless

00:06:55.250 --> 00:07:00.900
variety was in fact all the same. You say
it's difficult to describe? Indeed, that's

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:08.670
the point: It can't be described. It was everything
and anything, and something and nothing, and

00:07:08.670 --> 00:07:11.030
all-at-once and not-at-all.

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:17.120
What happened to it? Words, of course. It
started with a single, simple word, in a language

00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:22.850
that no one speaks anymore. No one knows where
it came from or how it sounded, but I'll tell

00:07:22.850 --> 00:07:29.389
you what it meant. It meant "red", and as
soon as there was "red" there was also "not-red".

00:07:29.389 --> 00:07:35.990
The world had been neatly cloven into red
any-every-somethings and not-red any-every-somethings.

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:42.680
It was the first division, and the very idea
of division spawned more ideas and more words:

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:50.729
"one", "two", "separate", "together", "us",
"them", and from these came more: many, many

00:07:50.729 --> 00:07:52.270
more.”

00:07:52.270 --> 00:07:56.840
The piece then goes on to reveal that the
proliferation of language destroyed this world

00:07:56.840 --> 00:08:03.449
further, until all that was left was the wreckage:
Our world. How a universe of infinite possibilities

00:08:03.449 --> 00:08:08.800
is slowly dulled by the ever-expanding power
of nomenclature. Truly a disturbing thought

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:10.620
for the more metaphysical among you.

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:15.180
Let’s take a look at another book from King
Shash’s collection. Next comes a frightening

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:20.680
parable about hubris from The Bard, known
as “Jack of Trades.” Like many a classic

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:26.960
parable, this piece tells the tale of an intelligent
spider - a Jack of Trades, as the story puts

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:32.380
it - whose arrogance forges him into the architect
of his own demise. Let’s take a look at

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:37.089
an extract, shall we?
“I once met a spider named Jack. A sizeable

00:08:37.089 --> 00:08:43.110
fellow he was, the size of my fist, and eight
legs as long as my hand. They were dexterous

00:08:43.110 --> 00:08:49.910
too, complementing his keen mind. That was
where his name came from—Jack of Trades,

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:53.320
for with each leg he would conduct each of
his trades.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:59.089
With one he was a blacksmith, forging steel-like
silk that with another he would spin into

00:08:59.089 --> 00:09:04.440
death traps. With a third he would contemplate
the design of his web, for he was an architect.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:09.110
With the fourth he would climb the tallest
of trees and bask in the daylight so that

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:12.800
all would see his splendour, and in it take
delight.

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:19.339
Oh yes, he was proud. Why wouldn’t he be?
He was the most talented spider to have ever

00:09:19.339 --> 00:09:23.730
graced the land, his crafts as numerous as
they were grand.

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:30.050
“I don’t just weave webs,” He said to
me, “I weave works of art, my traps instruments

00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:32.700
of death so much finer than all the rest.”

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:39.110
Then one day, Jack hatched a plan. Up the
tallest of trees he climbed. He did not rest

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:45.170
for two nights and two days, until he reached
the highest branch. The words some proclaim

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:51.040
say he could touch the moon up there, but
I digress, for one thing was certain; the

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:54.240
height he had reached was a thing to impress.

00:09:54.240 --> 00:09:59.870
What Jack did next was more august still.
Working each of his renowned legs he weaved

00:09:59.870 --> 00:10:05.029
a web from the base of the branch to its end.
So mighty in scale was it that he said it

00:10:05.029 --> 00:10:09.931
would catch him the sun, and make him master
of all that it touched.

00:10:09.931 --> 00:10:14.180
“It will bring me the moon too,” He told
me once, “and with the light of the brightest

00:10:14.180 --> 00:10:16.990
star I shall be master of the night.”

00:10:16.990 --> 00:10:22.100
Catch them he did, for as the sun climbed
it found itself twined in the mesh of Jack’s

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:26.690
web. As for the moon, it soon found itself
strewn with Jack's string, which clung to

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:32.920
its opalescent veneer as it drew near to its
ever-evasive lover, the bringer of day. Now

00:10:32.920 --> 00:10:39.160
they were tied to one another in Jack's trap
for him to reign over! It was thus he crafted

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:45.990
himself a throne of sunlight and moonlight,
the very heavens made to bow before his majesty.

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:48.570
Yet it was not to last.”

00:10:48.570 --> 00:10:53.100
The rest of the tale details the downfall
of Jack the Spider, as he devours the sun

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:57.820
and the moon above, driven by his boundless
hunger for greatness. Soon enough, he was

00:10:57.820 --> 00:11:03.900
left with nothing to consume, and in his despair,
tumbled from his web down to his doom. So

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:08.130
is often the way with those pushed by unchecked
ambition.

00:11:08.130 --> 00:11:13.620
Time to blow the dust off another one of the
Library’s forbidden tests. This one, entitled

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:18.680
“A Restless Wanderer On The Earth”, is
a particularly interesting piece for anyone

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:25.769
fascinated by the enigmatic life of SCP-073,
also known as Cain. This piece features a

00:11:25.769 --> 00:11:30.829
person named Bluebird buying a jar containing
Cain’s memories from a merchant - possibly

00:11:30.829 --> 00:11:35.640
affiliated with Marshall, Carter &amp; Dark Ltd.
It’s a complex tale concerning many small

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:41.100
fragments from Cain’s life, thousands of
years in the past. Here is a brief excerpt:

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:47.291
“The man found Cain ben Adam in the endless
wildernesses of the between-places. The man

00:11:47.291 --> 00:11:52.400
was beautiful to look upon, with bronzed skin
and shaggy black hair. His clothing was cut

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:58.529
of animal skin, of fashions pleasing to the
eye. But Cain was not pleased to see him.

00:11:58.529 --> 00:11:59.790
The man knew his name.

00:11:59.790 --> 00:12:05.029
"I am a priest," the man said. "A year ago,
you passed through my village. I wished to

00:12:05.029 --> 00:12:09.850
follow you then, but I was held back by the
others. Then the crop failed, and a plague

00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:15.579
swept through our village. Only I was able
to save myself, and then, there were none

00:12:15.579 --> 00:12:21.270
to hold me back. I have searched for you since,
and now I have finally found you."

00:12:21.270 --> 00:12:26.800
Cain frowned. He tried to wander only those
lands where the soil was wild and suffused

00:12:26.800 --> 00:12:31.160
with power enough to resist his curse, or
else lands where the inhabitants knew how

00:12:31.160 --> 00:12:34.540
to refresh the lands which his curse had made
barren.

00:12:34.540 --> 00:12:39.230
When he needed to pass through civilization,
he avoided the fields. He had long ago discovered

00:12:39.230 --> 00:12:44.040
that his curse had a limit — forty-eight
cubits around him, or a length similar to

00:12:44.040 --> 00:12:48.040
twelve times his own height. Enough to judge
with the naked eye.

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:53.860
But that did not account for the times he
was not fully aware. Often, taken with wanderlust,

00:12:53.860 --> 00:12:58.660
sometimes not even fully awake, he would walk
in a trance according to where his feet took

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:04.040
him alone. After these periods, he could awake
having destroyed the entire harvest for a

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:05.040
village.

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:07.540
"I am truly sorry," Cain said.

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:13.500
The man looked astonished. "You are Cain,
the Wanderer, father of the Lost Children

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:18.660
and the Great Beasts. You are the god of death.
The other gods, the lesser gods, could not

00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:26.180
save my village. I worshiped them once. I
despise them, now. I come now to worship you."

00:13:26.180 --> 00:13:31.810
"They were your family," Cain said. "Even
if they were not, I cannot be pleased about

00:13:31.810 --> 00:13:34.730
bringing more death to those who did not deserve
it."

00:13:34.730 --> 00:13:40.760
"You are testing me, my lord." The man's teeth
gleamed. "I promise you, I will not disappoint

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:44.279
you. I will become your follower, and your
priest!"

00:13:44.279 --> 00:13:47.430
"Foolish," Cain said, and continued walking.

00:13:47.430 --> 00:13:49.130
The man followed him.”

00:13:49.130 --> 00:13:53.300
It’s clear that the curse Cain is forced
to carry, since committing the terrible sin

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:57.830
of murder against his own brother, weighs
heavily upon him. And the powers he possesses

00:13:57.830 --> 00:14:03.050
now are both a blessing and a terrible detriment
to the normal life he would have led. As with

00:14:03.050 --> 00:14:07.130
anything involving Cain, it’s a deeply tragic
tale.

00:14:07.130 --> 00:14:13.300
Next comes another insight into a normally
secretive world. A piece entitled “The Exhibition”,

00:14:13.300 --> 00:14:18.850
written by an aspiring Anartist named Artifex.
The piece details his frustrating experience

00:14:18.850 --> 00:14:23.589
hosting his artwork at a private exhibition
hosted by the sinister members of Marshall,

00:14:23.589 --> 00:14:27.800
Carter, &amp; Dark Ltd. And of course, here is
a brief extract.

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:31.720
“I had never been so disappointed before.

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:39.470
Tonight was supposed to be my big night, the
anart exhibition that would define my career.

00:14:39.470 --> 00:14:45.470
Hosted by the richest men of the anomalous
world, Marshall, Carter &amp; Dark, luminaries

00:14:45.470 --> 00:14:49.490
from all across the multiverse had come to
see our work.

00:14:49.490 --> 00:14:55.740
I remembered how surprised and excited I was
to see the letter that I had even been accepted

00:14:55.740 --> 00:15:00.649
into the exhibition. To receive one of these
had been my dream since I was a child, learning

00:15:00.649 --> 00:15:05.480
all about the wonders of the anomalous on
my father's knee. The letter looked so formal,

00:15:05.480 --> 00:15:12.070
with the logo of MC&amp;D printed at the bottom,
that I thought it sealed my future forever.

00:15:12.070 --> 00:15:17.800
I spent five hours fretting beforehand. I
eventually settled on a suit to wear, a black-and-white

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:21.630
chessboard pattern, only with actual chess
pieces that moved and played games over the

00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:26.279
fabric. I had specifically requested that
it be made with some of the most famous games

00:15:26.279 --> 00:15:30.940
of history sewn into it, but when I looked
down at myself, I could not tell which game

00:15:30.940 --> 00:15:34.110
was which. Before I could second-guess my
choice of clothing, I looked at the time,

00:15:34.110 --> 00:15:38.250
and realised that I was running slightly late,
and left in a hurry, rushing to the portal

00:15:38.250 --> 00:15:42.139
that the letter told me to go, in between
a bookshop and a bowling alley.

00:15:42.139 --> 00:15:47.889
When I arrived, my astonishment at seeing
the place was a feeling I would never forget.

00:15:47.889 --> 00:15:53.190
It was ridiculously surreal, like if M. C.
Escher painted an art museum, and the museum

00:15:53.190 --> 00:15:58.140
was filled with Escher's paintings, with the
paintings themselves being alive. The chandeliers

00:15:58.140 --> 00:16:04.240
were made from glowing crystals, almost looking
more like disco balls than chandeliers. The

00:16:04.240 --> 00:16:08.850
crowd there was as impressive as the art,
with all sorts of people from all sorts of

00:16:08.850 --> 00:16:14.089
dimensions, dressed in the finest suits, capes
and cloaks that money could buy. As I approached

00:16:14.089 --> 00:16:19.170
the host, a tall, imposing figure, with grey
robes, a metal mask and a mane of white hair,

00:16:19.170 --> 00:16:21.170
I felt very intimidated.

00:16:21.170 --> 00:16:28.690
'Yes, yes, I've been awaiting your arrival,
Natura,' he was saying to a woman before him,

00:16:28.690 --> 00:16:33.360
a human with a green dress–that I quickly
noticed was actually made out of leaves–and

00:16:33.360 --> 00:16:37.699
dark green hair to match, flowing down past
her shoulders, with flowers in her hair and

00:16:37.699 --> 00:16:41.509
clothes. 'Your work is waiting for you in
the west wing.'

00:16:41.509 --> 00:16:46.610
The woman, who I assumed was named Natura,
eagerly nodded and looked over to her right.

00:16:46.610 --> 00:16:49.389
She started walking so quickly, she almost
bumped into me…”

00:16:49.389 --> 00:16:53.630
The night didn’t go much better for Artifex
after that. His anart pieces were a painting

00:16:53.630 --> 00:16:58.759
of a woman who makes you fall in love, a vase
that kills anything put inside it, and a little

00:16:58.759 --> 00:17:03.470
statue of an archer who fires real arrows.
The representatives of Marshall, Carter, &amp; Dark

00:17:03.470 --> 00:17:08.050
were not impressed by this meager display,
leaving Artifex - who’d mainly gotten in

00:17:08.050 --> 00:17:13.720
from his father’s business connections - feeling,
we’ll say, less than stoked.

00:17:13.720 --> 00:17:18.350
But the Wanderer’s Library isn’t all just
technical documents and prose. There’s also

00:17:18.350 --> 00:17:24.100
a fair share of spellbinding poetry in these
dusty old pages. Take, for example, “Stars

00:17:24.100 --> 00:17:30.110
wait for no man” by the illustrious poet
who goes only by Doctor Pann. It’s a powerful

00:17:30.110 --> 00:17:38.970
and melancholy piece, presented here, in full.
“The Abyss is an infinitude,

00:17:38.970 --> 00:17:42.600
Unknown and unknowable.
Creatures with mouths over throats

00:17:42.600 --> 00:17:48.520
Seek insight into their existence,
Gazing at that which only gazes back.

00:17:48.520 --> 00:17:52.310
They choke as they look,
Windpipes bent awkwardly,

00:17:52.310 --> 00:17:57.820
Tears trickling from their eyes.
Windows reflecting

00:17:57.820 --> 00:17:59.290
A goal, unachievable.

00:17:59.290 --> 00:18:04.750
Eyes peer into darkness
Searching for more and more,

00:18:04.750 --> 00:18:10.180
Yearning, hoping, desiring.
But not doing, never going,

00:18:10.180 --> 00:18:13.020
Weeping at the beauty beyond reach.

00:18:13.020 --> 00:18:16.270
A number tried
Reaching out to the void,

00:18:16.270 --> 00:18:19.970
But the others scoffed,
Drowning the doers with tears.

00:18:19.970 --> 00:18:24.370
All While they rested,
Their asses in the dirt.

00:18:24.370 --> 00:18:27.260
An eternity separates
That which seeks

00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:32.720
And that which is sought.
Eternity only grows as they gaze on,

00:18:32.720 --> 00:18:39.430
Dreaming, but not doing.
Stars wait for no man.”

00:18:39.430 --> 00:18:45.140
The Wanderer’s Library is a truly fascinating
place, with so much strange beauty and arcane

00:18:45.140 --> 00:18:49.360
knowledge to gather up and learn. Would you
like us to cover more of the many esoteric

00:18:49.360 --> 00:18:53.970
volumes in the Library’s infinite shelves?
Let us know down in the comments. And we thoroughly

00:18:53.970 --> 00:18:58.830
recommend you check out all the full tales
in the Library itself, using the link in the

00:18:58.830 --> 00:19:00.190
description.

00:19:00.190 --> 00:19:05.760
Just be careful when you do so. It’s easy
to get lost in there…

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:11.290
Now go check out “SCP-6000 - The Serpent,
the Moose and the Wanderer's Library” and

00:19:11.290 --> 00:19:16.500
“SCP Serpent's Hand Explained” for more
on the legendary library between dimensions!

